    <marker><name>01 Grouping and Windowing</name><comment>Grouping by all, share of total, running totals, moving averages and rank within a group — the family of calculations that need a row compared against its neighbours. Everything here answers a question of the form "this row, compared with…</comment><in>0</in><out>-1</out></marker>
    <marker><name>02 What you will learn</name><comment>- Group by all - Windowing - Partitioning is the part people get wrong - When a window is the wrong tool</comment><in>180</in><out>-1</out></marker>
    <marker><name>03 Group by all</name><comment>The simplest two-level calculation. group q by all produces one bucket containing everything, which gives you a company total to compare individual groups against. The two streams are then combined so each segment row can see the total.…</comment><in>480</in><out>-1</out></marker>
    <marker><name>04 Windowing</name><comment>A window function computes across a set of neighbouring rows without collapsing them. Three uses cover almost everything: A window without an explicit order is a bug that does not announce itself. Running totals, period-over-period and…</comment><in>1200</in><out>-1</out></marker>
    <marker><name>05 Partitioning is the part people get wrong</name><comment>Ranking reps within each region means the window resets per region. Ranking all reps globally and then filtering to one region gives you ranks 14, 27 and 31 rather than 1, 2 and 3 — arithmetically correct and wrong for the question. Say…</comment><in>1920</in><out>-1</out></marker>
    <marker><name>06 Example — Group by all</name><comment>SAQL example from "Group by all": ```saql -- revenue per segment, and each segment's share of the company total q = load "Academy_Opportunities"; q = filter q by 'StageName' == "Closed Won"; total = group q by all; total = foreach total…</comment><in>2640</in><out>-1</out></marker>
    <marker><name>07 Check yourself</name><comment>To compute each segment's share of company revenue you need: Answer: Each group's value compared against an aggregate of ALL rows — two levels of aggregation</comment><in>3000</in><out>-1</out></marker>
    <marker><name>08 Check yourself</name><comment>A windowed running total differs from a plain sum because: Answer: It accumulates across the ordered rows rather than collapsing them to one value</comment><in>3420</in><out>-1</out></marker>
    <marker><name>09 Check yourself</name><comment>Ranking reps within each region requires the window to be: Answer: Partitioned by region, so each region ranks from 1 independently</comment><in>3840</in><out>-1</out></marker>
    <marker><name>10 Recap</name><comment>- Group by all: The simplest two-level calculation. - Windowing: A window function computes across a set of neighbouring rows without collapsing them. - Partitioning is the part people get wrong: Ranking reps within each region means the…</comment><in>4260</in><out>-1</out></marker>
